WEBVTT

01-01
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.276
Welcome to Week five, from phases to chemical reactions.

01-02
00:00:04.456 --> 00:00:08.595
This is our independent lesson for Friday, November sixth.

01-03
00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:12.956
We will meet onsite again on Friday, November thirteenth.

01-04
00:00:13.136 --> 00:00:17.403
Please begin with the short review in section R of the worksheet.

01-05
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:23.044
Your recent questions showed that we should separate two ideas before
moving on.

01-06
00:00:23.224 --> 00:00:32.205
Bulk phase equilibrium concerns a substance distributed between
macroscopic phases, such as a solid and its vapor.

01-07
00:00:32.385 --> 00:00:36.375
Adsorption concerns uptake associated with a surface.

01-08
00:00:36.555 --> 00:00:43.104
The amount adsorbed depends on an adsorption relation and the
available surface capacity.

01-09
00:00:43.284 --> 00:00:47.252
A vapor pressure alone does not describe that uptake.

01-10
00:00:47.432 --> 00:00:55.961
Today, we will use the same habit of combining an equilibrium relation
with conservation, but the species can also react.

01-11
00:00:56.141 --> 00:01:01.709
Our main example is a synthetic dissociation, A two forming two A
atoms.

01-12
00:01:01.889 --> 00:01:05.431
We will follow one worksheet and use Lab twelve.

01-13
00:01:05.611 --> 00:01:10.923
Keep the worksheet beside the video so every pause has a clear place
to work.

02-01
00:01:13.453 --> 00:01:18.560
Before we discuss reactions, revisit pressure in a closed, rigid
vessel.

02-02
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.428
The temperature and the volume are fixed.

02-03
00:01:21.608 --> 00:01:27.923
The ideal gas equation connects the amount of each gas to its own
partial pressure.

02-04
00:01:28.103 --> 00:01:31.623
Total pressure is the sum of those partial pressures.

02-05
00:01:31.803 --> 00:01:38.928
If all of the volatile solid has disappeared, calculate the volatile
gas pressure from its available amount.

02-06
00:01:39.108 --> 00:01:46.255
Do not continue forcing its pressure to equal the saturation value
after that phase is depleted.

02-07
00:01:46.435 --> 00:01:50.851
The assumption of an ideal gas is a separate modeling choice.

02-08
00:01:51.031 --> 00:01:55.489
Losing the solid phase does not make a real gas ideal.

02-09
00:01:55.669 --> 00:01:59.104
Now pause for four minutes and complete R one.

02-10
00:01:59.284 --> 00:02:12.297
Use the values printed in the worksheet, calculate the volatile
partial pressure and total pressure, then state which pressure changes
when inert gas is added at fixed temperature and volume.

02-11
00:02:12.477 --> 00:02:15.443
Keep the volatile inventory unchanged.

02-12
00:02:15.623 --> 00:02:17.287
Record a unit check.

02-13
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:20.901
After your attempt, continue to the next question.

02-14
00:02:21.081 --> 00:02:23.961
We will check both review answers together.

03-01
00:02:26.491 --> 00:02:30.673
The next question concerns adsorption in a finite vessel.

03-02
00:02:30.853 --> 00:02:37.295
Conservation tells us that the feed is divided between free gas and
adsorbed material.

03-03
00:02:37.475 --> 00:02:43.427
The Langmuir equation supplies another condition that the proposed
state must satisfy.

03-04
00:02:43.607 --> 00:02:52.738
Choosing two amounts that add to the feed is insufficient if the
adsorbed amount disagrees with the isotherm at the same pressure.

03-05
00:02:52.918 --> 00:02:57.227
In R two, the pressure and the model parameters are supplied.

03-06
00:02:57.407 --> 00:03:02.186
First calculate the gas amount from pressure, volume and temperature.

03-07
00:03:02.366 --> 00:03:09.918
Independently calculate the loading from the Langmuir equation, then
multiply that loading by the adsorbent mass.

03-08
00:03:10.098 --> 00:03:15.154
Only after these two calculations should you compare their sum with
the feed.

03-09
00:03:15.334 --> 00:03:21.265
Use units of mole per kilogram for the loading and kilograms for the
adsorbent mass.

03-10
00:03:21.445 --> 00:03:24.111
Their product is an amount in moles.

03-11
00:03:24.291 --> 00:03:27.299
Pause for four minutes and complete R two.

03-12
00:03:27.479 --> 00:03:33.261
If a number seems unreasonable, write which equation or conversion you
need to check.

03-13
00:03:33.441 --> 00:03:36.043
Continue when the time budget ends.

04-01
00:03:38.573 --> 00:03:40.237
Check R one first.

04-02
00:03:40.417 --> 00:03:51.041
The gas capacity at the sublimation pressure is about four thousandths
of a mole, which exceeds the available zero point zero zero two moles.

04-03
00:03:51.221 --> 00:03:53.312
All of the solid disappears.

04-04
00:03:53.492 --> 00:03:58.996
The volatile partial pressure is about four point nine eight nine
kilopascals.

04-05
00:03:59.176 --> 00:04:12.851
Adding the specified inert gas at fixed temperature and volume raises
total pressure to about nine point nine seven seven kilopascals, while
the volatile partial pressure stays unchanged.

04-06
00:04:13.031 --> 00:04:14.759
Now check R two.

04-07
00:04:14.939 --> 00:04:26.253
At the proposed ten kilopascals, the gas contains about four
thousandths of a mole, and the isotherm requires zero point zero one
moles on the surface.

04-08
00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:28.374
Their sum exceeds the feed.

04-09
00:04:28.554 --> 00:04:33.930
That proposed pressure cannot satisfy this model and inventory
together.

04-10
00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:39.870
The checked equilibrium pressure is about six point zero eight zero
kilopascals.

04-11
00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:49.799
It gives about zero point zero zero two four four moles of gas and
zero point zero zero seven five six moles adsorbed.

04-12
00:04:49.979 --> 00:04:51.857
The sum matches the feed.

04-13
00:04:52.037 --> 00:04:58.245
We will use the same combination of equilibrium and conservation
checks for reactions.

05-01
00:05:00.775 --> 00:05:03.463
Here is the complete route for this week.

05-02
00:05:03.643 --> 00:05:07.931
Use the six-page Week five worksheet as your working record.

05-03
00:05:08.111 --> 00:05:12.505
Keep Lab twelve open in a second tab when we reach section D.

05-04
00:05:12.685 --> 00:05:17.208
The short Lab guide is available when you need help finding a control.

05-05
00:05:17.388 --> 00:05:20.588
The worked solutions are for checking after an attempt.

05-06
00:05:20.768 --> 00:05:25.824
There is no additional required preparation or follow-up assignment.

05-07
00:05:26.004 --> 00:05:31.913
The planned activities use one hundred and twenty-eight minutes, with
a separate ten-minute break.

05-08
00:05:32.093 --> 00:05:38.429
Add the recording duration shown on the video page for a total of
approximately three hours.

05-09
00:05:38.609 --> 00:05:40.807
Your actual time may differ.

05-10
00:05:40.987 --> 00:05:44.016
Please use the suggested stop budgets.

05-11
00:05:44.196 --> 00:05:49.551
If you remain stuck at the end of a pause, record the specific step
and continue.

05-12
00:05:49.731 --> 00:05:54.189
Bring that question to our onsite class on November thirteenth.

05-13
00:05:54.369 --> 00:05:57.847
You do not need to repeat the whole worksheet neatly.

05-14
00:05:58.027 --> 00:06:04.128
A first attempt, a correction and a short explanation provide a useful
record of your learning.

06-01
00:06:06.658 --> 00:06:11.010
Our reaction is A two gas in equilibrium with two A gas.

06-02
00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.326
We begin with one mole of A two and no A.

06-03
00:06:14.506 --> 00:06:18.474
No inert gas is present in the three required cases.

06-04
00:06:18.654 --> 00:06:26.718
A two contains two A atoms, so the reaction conserves atoms even
though the number of gas molecules changes.

06-05
00:06:26.898 --> 00:06:33.682
Reaction extent tells us how far the reaction has proceeded on the
written stoichiometric basis.

06-06
00:06:33.862 --> 00:06:37.574
Consuming one mole of A two produces two moles of A.

06-07
00:06:37.754 --> 00:06:45.370
The general expression is the initial amount plus the stoichiometric
coefficient times the reaction extent.

06-08
00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:50.777
Reactant coefficients are negative and product coefficients are
positive.

06-09
00:06:50.957 --> 00:06:58.253
For this particular feed, it is convenient to define f as the reaction
extent divided by one mole.

06-10
00:06:58.433 --> 00:07:00.865
The fraction f is dimensionless.

06-11
00:07:01.045 --> 00:07:05.994
The amounts are one minus f moles of A two and two f moles of A.

06-12
00:07:06.174 --> 00:07:10.398
Nonnegative amounts require f between zero and one.

06-13
00:07:10.578 --> 00:07:16.697
These bounds come from the available material, before we use any
equilibrium constant.

07-01
00:07:19.227 --> 00:07:22.576
To describe equilibrium, we need activities.

07-02
00:07:22.756 --> 00:07:33.163
For today's ideal gas model, the activity of a species is its mole
fraction multiplied by total pressure, divided by the standard
pressure.

07-03
00:07:33.343 --> 00:07:35.924
Both pressures must use the same units.

07-04
00:07:36.104 --> 00:07:40.157
Their ratio is dimensionless, and so is the activity.

07-05
00:07:40.337 --> 00:07:45.713
We use a standard pressure of one hundred kilopascals throughout this
lesson.

07-06
00:07:45.893 --> 00:07:48.091
This is a reference convention.

07-07
00:07:48.271 --> 00:07:52.943
The actual total pressure is a separate input that we can change.

07-08
00:07:53.123 --> 00:08:01.059
For A two dissociating into two A, the quotient is the activity of A
squared, divided by the activity of A two.

07-09
00:08:01.239 --> 00:08:05.036
The exponents come directly from the reaction coefficients.

07-10
00:08:05.216 --> 00:08:09.909
Write this activity expression before substituting mole fractions.

07-11
00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:14.612
That step makes it easier to see how pressure enters the calculation.

07-12
00:08:14.792 --> 00:08:20.488
Later we will compare one hundred and four hundred kilopascals at the
same temperature.

07-13
00:08:20.668 --> 00:08:25.703
Our standard pressure stays at one hundred kilopascals in both cases.

08-01
00:08:28.233 --> 00:08:32.265
Please pause for ten minutes and complete A one through A three.

08-02
00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:34.322
Begin with a mole table.

08-03
00:08:34.502 --> 00:08:39.003
Write the amounts of A two, A and the total gas in terms of f.

08-04
00:08:39.183 --> 00:08:44.773
Divide each species amount by the total amount to obtain its mole
fraction.

08-05
00:08:44.953 --> 00:08:47.193
Check that the fractions sum to one.

08-06
00:08:47.373 --> 00:08:53.922
Substitute those fractions into the activity definition and then into
the reaction quotient.

08-07
00:08:54.102 --> 00:08:57.899
Try to simplify the expression without looking ahead.

08-08
00:08:58.079 --> 00:09:02.239
Keep the actual-pressure to standard-pressure ratio visible.

08-09
00:09:02.419 --> 00:09:11.742
Evaluate the quotient at a trial fraction of zero point five, first at
a pressure ratio of one and then at a ratio of four.

08-10
00:09:11.922 --> 00:09:17.213
Finally, check the total number of A atoms using the stoichiometric
counts.

08-11
00:09:17.393 --> 00:09:25.175
The total molecular amount can change, but the atom inventory must
remain equal to the initial inventory.

08-12
00:09:25.355 --> 00:09:34.145
If the algebra becomes difficult, retain the unsimplified expression
and substitute one trial value of f to test it.

08-13
00:09:34.325 --> 00:09:37.119
That is a useful intermediate result.

08-14
00:09:37.299 --> 00:09:42.675
At the end of ten minutes, record any unfinished step and continue to
the check.

08-15
00:09:42.855 --> 00:09:45.415
The next slide shows the derivation.

09-01
00:09:47.945 --> 00:09:51.359
The total gas amount is one plus f moles.

09-02
00:09:51.539 --> 00:09:57.128
Therefore, the mole fraction of A two is one minus f, divided by one
plus f.

09-03
00:09:57.308 --> 00:10:01.660
The mole fraction of A is two f, divided by one plus f.

09-04
00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:11.120
Substituting these into the quotient gives four f squared divided by
one minus f squared, multiplied by the pressure ratio.

09-05
00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:19.961
One pressure factor remains because the product side has one more mole
of gas per mole of reaction than the reactant side.

09-06
00:10:20.141 --> 00:10:27.587
If your expression has two pressure factors or none, return to the
activity exponents and cancel them explicitly.

09-07
00:10:27.767 --> 00:10:32.204
The atom check is twice the amount of A two plus the amount of A.

09-08
00:10:32.384 --> 00:10:37.675
Substitution gives two moles of conserved A atoms for every feasible
f.

09-09
00:10:37.855 --> 00:10:43.359
Notice that molecular moles and atom moles describe different
inventories.

09-10
00:10:43.539 --> 00:10:56.253
At a trial fraction of zero point five, the quotient is one point
three three three three at a pressure ratio of one, and five point
three three three three at a pressure ratio of four.

09-11
00:10:56.433 --> 00:11:01.447
These are trial states, so we have not yet established equilibrium.

09-12
00:11:01.627 --> 00:11:06.533
The quotient changes with f, while the atom balance remains satisfied.

10-01
00:11:09.063 --> 00:11:12.669
How can the quotient tell us which direction is favored?

10-02
00:11:12.849 --> 00:11:23.771
At fixed temperature and pressure, the reaction Gibbs energy is the
standard reaction Gibbs energy plus R T times the natural logarithm of
Q.

10-03
00:11:23.951 --> 00:11:30.287
An equivalent form is R T times the natural logarithm of Q divided by
K.

10-04
00:11:30.467 --> 00:11:33.646
A negative value favors forward reaction.

10-05
00:11:33.826 --> 00:11:37.111
A positive value favors reverse reaction.

10-06
00:11:37.291 --> 00:11:43.755
At an interior equilibrium, the reaction Gibbs energy is zero and Q
equals K.

10-07
00:11:43.935 --> 00:11:47.199
These statements refer to the reaction as written.

10-08
00:11:47.379 --> 00:11:51.838
Reversing the reaction reverses the corresponding sign convention.

10-09
00:11:52.018 --> 00:12:01.725
For B one, use the supplied standard reaction Gibbs energy of minus
four thousand joules per mole at three hundred and fifty kelvin.

10-10
00:12:01.905 --> 00:12:05.361
Test Q equal to one and Q equal to ten.

10-11
00:12:05.541 --> 00:12:11.514
Pause for six minutes, calculate the signs and write the favored
direction for each state.

10-12
00:12:11.694 --> 00:12:13.998
Use the natural logarithm.

10-13
00:12:14.178 --> 00:12:19.213
We will return to these answers after calculating K by two routes.

11-01
00:12:21.743 --> 00:12:27.737
The first route starts with standard reaction Gibbs energy at the
temperature of interest.

11-02
00:12:27.917 --> 00:12:35.704
The equation is natural log K equals minus standard reaction Gibbs
energy divided by R T.

11-03
00:12:35.884 --> 00:12:43.223
For this example, the supplied standard reaction Gibbs energy applies
at three hundred and fifty kelvin.

11-04
00:12:43.403 --> 00:12:47.733
It is minus four kilojoules per mole of reaction as written.

11-05
00:12:47.913 --> 00:12:56.063
To use the gas constant in joules per mole per kelvin, convert that
energy to minus four thousand joules per mole.

11-06
00:12:56.243 --> 00:13:00.232
Keep the two minus signs separate until you substitute.

11-07
00:13:00.412 --> 00:13:08.732
A negative standard reaction Gibbs energy gives a positive natural
logarithm of K, so K should exceed one.

11-08
00:13:08.912 --> 00:13:12.837
This sign prediction is a quick check on the arithmetic.

11-09
00:13:13.017 --> 00:13:20.740
The final equilibrium constant is dimensionless because it is defined
using dimensionless activities.

11-10
00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:29.482
A larger K describes a stronger product preference in this convention,
but it does not by itself specify the conversion.

11-11
00:13:29.662 --> 00:13:32.691
The feed and constraints are also needed.

12-01
00:13:35.221 --> 00:13:44.139
The second route constructs standard reaction Gibbs energy from
standard reaction enthalpy and entropy at the same temperature.

12-02
00:13:44.319 --> 00:13:49.524
For the reference case, the enthalpy is fifty kilojoules per mole of
reaction.

12-03
00:13:49.704 --> 00:13:56.979
The entropy is about one hundred and fifty-four point two eight five
seven joules per mole per kelvin.

12-04
00:13:57.159 --> 00:14:03.175
Convert either the energy or entropy so that the subtraction uses
consistent units.

12-05
00:14:03.355 --> 00:14:10.843
In joules, calculate fifty thousand minus three hundred and fifty
multiplied by the entropy value.

12-06
00:14:11.023 --> 00:14:15.780
Then use that Gibbs energy in the equation for natural log K.

12-07
00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:21.635
These two routes should agree because the synthetic inputs were
constructed consistently.

12-08
00:14:21.815 --> 00:14:31.647
In an experimental or literature calculation, agreement also depends
on using the same phases, standard states and reaction basis.

12-09
00:14:31.827 --> 00:14:38.525
If the routes disagree, check the inputs before interpreting the
discrepancy as physical behavior.

12-10
00:14:38.705 --> 00:14:46.172
For today's comparison, keep all supplied properties at the reference
temperature of three hundred and fifty kelvin.

13-01
00:14:48.702 --> 00:14:52.585
Pause for ten minutes and complete B two through B four.

13-02
00:14:52.765 --> 00:14:59.143
Calculate natural log K and then K from the directly supplied standard
Gibbs energy.

13-03
00:14:59.323 --> 00:15:07.317
Next, calculate standard Gibbs energy from enthalpy and entropy, and
use that value to obtain a second K.

13-04
00:15:07.497 --> 00:15:12.723
Show the energy conversion on your worksheet so someone else can
follow your units.

13-05
00:15:12.903 --> 00:15:18.493
Keep several digits during the calculation and round only the reported
result.

13-06
00:15:18.673 --> 00:15:24.177
Compare the two routes and write why agreement is expected for this
synthetic case.

13-07
00:15:24.357 --> 00:15:31.909
If you obtain a reciprocal value or a very small constant, revisit the
minus sign in the exponential.

13-08
00:15:32.089 --> 00:15:38.958
If your two Gibbs energies differ by a factor of a thousand, check the
joule and kilojoule conversion.

13-09
00:15:39.138 --> 00:15:43.383
You can use a calculator or a spreadsheet for the arithmetic.

13-10
00:15:43.563 --> 00:15:50.198
The objective is to make the route from the supplied properties to the
equilibrium constant visible.

13-11
00:15:50.378 --> 00:15:53.941
Continue to the numerical check after your attempt.

14-01
00:15:56.471 --> 00:16:04.961
Both routes give a standard reaction Gibbs energy of minus four
thousand joules per mole at three hundred and fifty kelvin.

14-02
00:16:05.141 --> 00:16:13.653
Dividing its negative by R T gives natural log K equal to about one
point three seven four five four.

14-03
00:16:13.833 --> 00:16:19.423
Exponentiating gives K equal to about three point nine five three two
six.

14-04
00:16:19.603 --> 00:16:30.781
The enthalpy-entropy calculation reaches the same result because fifty
thousand minus fifty-four thousand equals minus four thousand joules
per mole.

14-05
00:16:30.961 --> 00:16:33.180
Return briefly to B one.

14-06
00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:44.432
When Q is one, its natural logarithm is zero, so the reaction Gibbs
energy is minus four kilojoules per mole and forward reaction is
favored.

14-07
00:16:44.612 --> 00:16:47.298
When Q is ten, Q exceeds K.

14-08
00:16:47.478 --> 00:16:53.025
The logarithm of Q over K is positive, so reverse reaction is favored.

14-09
00:16:53.205 --> 00:16:56.640
At equilibrium, Q equals this finite K.

14-10
00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:02.452
The standard reaction Gibbs energy remains minus four kilojoules per
mole.

14-11
00:17:02.632 --> 00:17:07.005
It is the actual reaction Gibbs energy that becomes zero.

15-01
00:17:09.535 --> 00:17:12.714
Now use K to calculate composition.

15-02
00:17:12.894 --> 00:17:17.779
At equilibrium, set the quotient expression from section A equal to K.

15-03
00:17:17.959 --> 00:17:29.474
If r denotes the actual-pressure to standard-pressure ratio, the
equation is K equals four f squared times r, divided by one minus f
squared.

15-04
00:17:29.654 --> 00:17:35.286
Rearranging gives f squared equal to K divided by K plus four r.

15-05
00:17:35.466 --> 00:17:43.508
Take the nonnegative root because our initial feed allows only forward
extent between zero and one mole.

15-06
00:17:43.688 --> 00:17:48.531
For the reference pressure of one hundred kilopascals, r equals one.

15-07
00:17:48.711 --> 00:17:52.850
Pause for fifteen minutes and complete C one through C three.

15-08
00:17:53.030 --> 00:18:00.852
Calculate f, convert it to reaction extent, and calculate both species
amounts and their mole fractions.

15-09
00:18:01.032 --> 00:18:03.443
Then verify the atom balance.

15-10
00:18:03.623 --> 00:18:08.935
Reconstruct Q from the calculated mole fractions and the pressure
ratio.

15-11
00:18:09.115 --> 00:18:13.083
This second calculation should return K within rounding.

15-12
00:18:13.263 --> 00:18:17.956
Please do not use the rearranged equation as your only check.

15-13
00:18:18.136 --> 00:18:22.424
The purpose is to test the final composition independently.

16-01
00:18:24.954 --> 00:18:29.733
For the reference case, f is about zero point seven zero five.

16-02
00:18:29.913 --> 00:18:35.140
The reaction extent is therefore about zero point seven zero five
moles.

16-03
00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:45.074
The remaining A two amount is about zero point two nine five moles,
and the A amount is about one point four one zero moles.

16-04
00:18:45.254 --> 00:18:49.115
Their sum is about one point seven zero five moles.

16-05
00:18:49.295 --> 00:18:58.596
Dividing by this total gives mole fractions of about zero point one
seven three for A two and zero point eight two seven for A.

16-06
00:18:58.776 --> 00:19:04.600
These fractions differ from the species amounts, even though both
describe the same state.

16-07
00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:10.071
Use the more precise on-screen values when checking numerical closure.

16-08
00:19:10.251 --> 00:19:13.344
The atom check gives two moles of A atoms.

16-09
00:19:13.524 --> 00:19:23.679
At this pressure, the activities equal the mole fractions numerically,
so the activity quotient returns about three point nine five three two
six.

16-10
00:19:23.859 --> 00:19:30.472
K greater than one has produced substantial dissociation, while a
finite amount of A two remains.

17-01
00:19:33.002 --> 00:19:40.874
Next, increase total pressure to four hundred kilopascals while
keeping temperature at three hundred and fifty kelvin.

17-02
00:19:41.054 --> 00:19:43.998
Keep the same feed and standard pressure.

17-03
00:19:44.178 --> 00:19:49.469
Before calculating, predict whether dissociation should increase or
decrease.

17-04
00:19:49.649 --> 00:19:54.428
The reaction produces more gas molecules in the forward direction.

17-05
00:19:54.608 --> 00:19:59.429
At a fixed composition, increasing pressure therefore increases Q.

17-06
00:19:59.609 --> 00:20:06.265
Use that observation to predict which way the system must react to
recover Q equal to K.

17-07
00:20:06.445 --> 00:20:11.202
Then calculate the new equilibrium using r equal to four.

17-08
00:20:11.382 --> 00:20:14.348
Pause for eight minutes and complete C four.

17-09
00:20:14.528 --> 00:20:18.645
Report the new extent, species amounts and mole fractions.

17-10
00:20:18.825 --> 00:20:26.057
Write one sentence explaining what happens to K and one sentence
explaining what happens to composition.

17-11
00:20:26.237 --> 00:20:29.842
Use the temperature condition in your explanation.

17-12
00:20:30.022 --> 00:20:38.300
It is possible for equilibrium composition to change while the
standard equilibrium constant remains the same.

17-13
00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:44.837
This controlled comparison will help you distinguish those two
quantities when you open the Lab.

18-01
00:20:47.367 --> 00:20:58.012
At four hundred kilopascals, K remains about three point nine five
three two six because temperature and the standard-state convention
are unchanged.

18-02
00:20:58.192 --> 00:21:04.251
The equilibrium extent decreases to about zero point four four five
one moles.

18-03
00:21:04.431 --> 00:21:14.710
The A two amount becomes about zero point five five four nine moles,
and the A amount becomes about zero point eight nine zero two moles.

18-04
00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:25.147
The A mole fraction is about zero point six one six zero three, and
the A two mole fraction is about zero point three eight three nine
seven.

18-05
00:21:25.327 --> 00:21:30.042
The higher-pressure mixture therefore contains less dissociated A.

18-06
00:21:30.222 --> 00:21:38.755
You can explain this directly from the quotient: at a fixed f,
multiplying pressure by four multiplies Q by four.

18-07
00:21:38.935 --> 00:21:43.351
Reverse reaction then reduces f until Q returns to K.

18-08
00:21:43.531 --> 00:21:50.144
This is more precise than applying a pressure slogan without
specifying the reaction or constraint.

18-09
00:21:50.324 --> 00:21:55.572
Keep the baseline and pressure-only cases beside each other in your
worksheet.

18-10
00:21:55.752 --> 00:22:01.555
The atom total should be two in both cases, even though the molecular
totals differ.

18-11
00:22:01.735 --> 00:22:07.900
We will now reproduce these states using the same ideal gas model in
Lab twelve.

19-01
00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:15.124
Open Lab twelve using the link on this slide or the video page.

19-02
00:22:15.304 --> 00:22:19.186
