23-06
00:28:54.143 --> 00:29:01.610
Pause the video, step away from the screen if you can, and resume at
the temperature section after about ten minutes.

23-07
00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:04.734
The break is included in the lesson budget.

24-01
00:29:07.264 --> 00:29:10.378
We have changed pressure while holding K fixed.

24-02
00:29:10.558 --> 00:29:12.542
Next we change temperature.

24-03
00:29:12.722 --> 00:29:20.466
The standard equilibrium constant depends on temperature for a fixed
reaction and standard-state convention.

24-04
00:29:20.646 --> 00:29:26.577
A Gibbs-energy value supplied at one temperature gives one point on
that relationship.

24-05
00:29:26.757 --> 00:29:30.853
It does not automatically give a law for other temperatures.

24-06
00:29:31.033 --> 00:29:41.380
To estimate K at four hundred kelvin, we will use the reference
constant at three hundred and fifty kelvin together with the standard
reaction enthalpy.

24-07
00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:47.256
Our model assumes that the reaction enthalpy remains constant over
this interval.

24-08
00:29:47.436 --> 00:29:52.961
This is an approximation with a stated range, rather than a new
measured data set.

24-09
00:29:53.141 --> 00:29:58.730
Because the reaction enthalpy is positive, the dissociation is
endothermic.

24-10
00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:06.590
Before using the equation, predict whether a higher temperature should
increase or decrease the equilibrium constant.

24-11
00:30:06.770 --> 00:30:14.109
Keep pressure at one hundred kilopascals for the temperature case so
the comparison isolates temperature.

24-12
00:30:14.289 --> 00:30:18.534
The initial feed and standard pressure also remain unchanged.

25-01
00:30:21.064 --> 00:30:31.283
The integrated van't Hoff equation relates the natural logarithm of
the ratio of the two equilibrium constants to the reaction enthalpy.

25-02
00:30:31.463 --> 00:30:41.980
With constant enthalpy, it is the enthalpy divided by R, multiplied by
one over the reference temperature minus one over the new temperature.

25-03
00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:49.696
Here the reference temperature is three hundred and fifty kelvin, and
the new temperature is four hundred kelvin.

25-04
00:30:49.876 --> 00:30:54.292
Use fifty thousand joules per mole for the reaction enthalpy.

25-05
00:30:54.472 --> 00:31:01.725
Since the new temperature is higher, one over the reference
temperature is larger than one over the new temperature.

25-06
00:31:01.905 --> 00:31:03.846
The difference is positive.

25-07
00:31:04.026 --> 00:31:10.298
The enthalpy is also positive, so the logarithm of the K ratio should
be positive.

25-08
00:31:10.478 --> 00:31:12.761
That predicts an increase in K.

25-09
00:31:12.941 --> 00:31:19.746
To find the new natural logarithm of K, add this change to the
reference natural logarithm.

25-10
00:31:19.926 --> 00:31:21.953
Then exponentiate once.

25-11
00:31:22.133 --> 00:31:28.170
Keep both temperatures in kelvin and check the sign before calculating
the final value.

26-01
00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:34.626
Pause for fifteen minutes and complete E one through E three.

26-02
00:31:34.806 --> 00:31:44.427
Start with the constant-enthalpy van't Hoff equation and substitute
the reference temperature, new temperature and enthalpy with their
units.

26-03
00:31:44.607 --> 00:31:47.615
Calculate the logarithmic change first.

26-04
00:31:47.795 --> 00:31:54.323
Add it to the reference natural logarithm of K, then obtain K at four
hundred kelvin.

26-05
00:31:54.503 --> 00:32:04.444
Cross-check the new Gibbs energy with enthalpy minus the new
temperature times entropy, under the same zero heat-capacity-change
assumption.

26-06
00:32:04.624 --> 00:32:10.896
Then use the new K in the equilibrium-extent expression at a pressure
ratio of one.

26-07
00:32:11.076 --> 00:32:16.687
Calculate the species amounts and make the same two independent checks
as before.

26-08
00:32:16.867 --> 00:32:24.888
The atom total should remain unchanged, and the quotient reconstructed
from the final composition should match the new K.

26-09
00:32:25.068 --> 00:32:29.399
Keep enough intermediate digits to avoid a rounding mismatch.

26-10
00:32:29.579 --> 00:32:37.067
Write a short explanation connecting the positive reaction enthalpy to
the direction of the temperature effect.

26-11
00:32:37.247 --> 00:32:43.220
If you need more time, mark the specific unfinished line and continue
to the solution.

26-12
00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:49.310
You can return to that line during our onsite question period on
November thirteenth.

27-01
00:32:51.840 --> 00:33:00.458
The logarithmic change from three hundred and fifty to four hundred
kelvin is about two point one four seven seven two.

27-02
00:33:00.638 --> 00:33:06.740
Adding the reference natural logarithm gives about three point five
two two two six.

27-03
00:33:06.920 --> 00:33:13.469
The new equilibrium constant is therefore about thirty-three point
eight six zero nine three.

27-04
00:33:13.649 --> 00:33:25.382
The enthalpy-entropy cross-check gives standard reaction Gibbs energy
of about minus eleven point seven one four three kilojoules per mole
at four hundred kelvin.

27-05
00:33:25.562 --> 00:33:33.050
At one hundred kilopascals, this gives an extent of about zero point
nine four five seven zero moles.

27-06
00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:43.732
The A two amount is about zero point zero five four three zero moles,
and the A amount is about one point eight nine one four zero moles.

27-07
00:33:43.912 --> 00:33:53.597
The resulting mole fractions are about zero point nine seven two zero
nine for A and zero point zero two seven nine one for A two.

27-08
00:33:53.777 --> 00:34:00.156
Heating has increased dissociation for this endothermic reaction under
the stated model.

27-09
00:34:00.336 --> 00:34:04.283
The atom inventory still equals two moles of A atoms.

27-10
00:34:04.463 --> 00:34:13.380
Compare this case with the reference case, not with the four-hundred-
kilopascal case, when attributing the difference to temperature alone.

27-11
00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:17.699
The increase in K is a property of the temperature model.

27-12
00:34:17.879 --> 00:34:22.551
The increase in extent also uses the specified feed and pressure.

28-01
00:34:25.081 --> 00:34:34.275
At four hundred kelvin, the direct Gibbs and formation-energy rows
supplied at three hundred and fifty kelvin become unavailable.

28-02
00:34:34.455 --> 00:34:36.055
That is appropriate.

28-03
00:34:36.235 --> 00:34:42.358
Changing a temperature label would not create valid property data at a
new temperature.

28-04
00:34:42.538 --> 00:34:45.674
Use the temperature-dependent route instead.

28-05
00:34:45.854 --> 00:34:55.149
For our required calculation, select van't Hoff with constant reaction
enthalpy, and keep the heat-capacity change at zero.

28-06
00:34:55.329 --> 00:34:58.827
The Lab also offers a heat-capacity correction.

28-07
00:34:59.007 --> 00:35:05.685
With its default nonzero value, that is a different temperature model
and can give a different K.

28-08
00:35:05.865 --> 00:35:10.895
You may explore it later, but it is outside this week's required work.

28-09
00:35:11.075 --> 00:35:16.750
The independent observed-composition panel has its own observation
temperature.

28-10
00:35:16.930 --> 00:35:28.855
A three-hundred-and-fifty-kelvin observation does not become a four-
hundred-kelvin observation simply because the main evaluation
temperature changes.

28-11
00:35:29.035 --> 00:35:38.209
Its unavailable message does not prevent the equilibrium extent
calculation from using an applicable thermodynamic route.

28-12
00:35:38.389 --> 00:35:43.359
Read each panel's temperature and label before comparing its output.

